add('consulting-business-plan', 'A one-page consulting plan you can actually test',
    'Add a copyable plan structure and reconcile acquisition assumptions to the three-project delivery model.', '''
<p>Use the existing three-project model as the financial appendix to a short written plan. The sales assumptions must support those three projects without using the same opportunity twice. The following funnel is a separate fictional acquisition test, not a benchmark or a prediction.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>One-page consulting plan: evidence to record</caption><thead><tr><th>Plan section</th><th>Specific entry to prepare</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Buyer and problem</td><td>One buyer role, business type and costly problem; retain dated interview notes.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Offer and alternative</td><td>Named deliverables, exclusions, acceptance evidence and the alternative the buyer uses today.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sales test</td><td>30 qualified prospects → 12 calls → 6 proposals → 3 signed projects.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Delivery</td><td>The 92 committed hours above, the 12-hour buffer and a change-request owner.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Money and decision</td><td>Startup outlays, dated collections, payment commitments and the evidence needed to continue.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>The assumed stage conversions are 40%, 50% and 50%: 30 × 0.40 × 0.50 × 0.50 = 3 projects. Six proposals at a 25% win rate would produce an expectation of 1.5 wins, not three bookings. Keep actual signed work separate from probability-weighted forecasts, and avoid spending against fractional expected clients.</p>
<p>Use the <a href="/tools/business-plan-template/">editable business plan template</a> to capture the narrative. If the sales test succeeds but hours overrun, revise the <a href="/journal/agency-capacity-planning-example/">role and delivery-capacity plan</a> before promising a fourth project. A lender version may need a fuller financial package; the <a href="https://www.sba.gov/business-guide/plan-your-business/write-your-business-plan" rel="noopener">SBA plan outline</a> helps identify that additional scope.</p>''')

add('spa-business-plan', 'Find the completed-appointment threshold before adding rooms',
    'Extend the existing day economics into a whole-appointment threshold and startup evidence checklist.', '''
<p>The day model above gives a useful minimum-volume test. At the fictional $90 service price, $17 of consumables and laundry and a 3% payment fee leave $70.30 per completed appointment before the $400 of listed scheduled-day costs. The threshold is $400 ÷ $70.30 = 5.69, so at least <strong>six completed appointments</strong> are needed to cover those listed costs.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Completed appointments, using the same cost boundary</caption><thead><tr><th>Completed appointments</th><th>Contribution before day costs</th><th>After $400 day costs</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>5</td><td>$351.50</td><td>−$48.50</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>$421.80</td><td>$21.80</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>$492.10</td><td>$92.10</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Six is not the whole-business break-even point: owner compensation, financing, tax and unlisted costs are still excluded. With eight booked slots, two missed appointments leave six completed; three missed appointments leave five. A full diary and a completed-service count therefore answer different questions.</p>
<p>Before committing to premises, attach supplier quotes for fit-out and equipment, service-specific permissions, qualified staff coverage, a local customer test and a dated opening-cash schedule. Separate deposits and refundable balances from freely available startup funding. Use the <a href="/tools/business-startup-checklist/">startup evidence checklist</a> to assign each unresolved item an owner, then put actual payments in the <a href="/journal/cash-flow-forecast-template-example/">13-week cash forecast</a>. Room expansion should follow a demonstrated constraint, not merely an attractive appointment target.</p>''')

add('hotel-business-plan', 'Compare booking channels at the same room revenue',
    'Add channel-level contribution and an actionable hotel plan outline rather than only blended fees.', '''
<p>The 8% blended distribution/payment assumption above is convenient for a first model, but it hides channel mix. Test the same 198 sold room-nights at the same $120 rate using two separate fictional fee assumptions. These figures are not hotel-platform prices.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Separate channel sensitivity; room revenue stays $23,760</caption><thead><tr><th>Scenario</th><th>Distribution/payment costs</th><th>After $4,356 variable and $15,000 period costs</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>All nights at an assumed 4% fee</td><td>$950.40</td><td>$3,453.60</td></tr><tr><td>All nights at an assumed 12% fee</td><td>$2,851.20</td><td>$1,552.80</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>The $1,900.80 difference is a fee sensitivity, not a recommendation to abandon a channel. Direct acquisition, staffing, cancellations, refunds and the demand a channel brings can change the comparison. If a fee excludes processing or tax, add the missing component once and label the boundary.</p>
<p>Build the written plan around guest segment, dated local demand evidence, room inventory, channel terms, staffing, maintenance, startup funding and downside response. Link each claim to a quote, observation or operating record. Use the <a href="/tools/business-plan-template/">business plan worksheet</a> for the narrative and the <a href="/journal/operations-dashboard-example/">operations dashboard example</a> for the ongoing exception review. Keep future bookings, completed stays and money settled by channels as separate records.</p>''')

add('operations-dashboard-example', 'Measure overdue promises even when the jobs stay open',
    'Work through the due-cohort denominator explicitly identified but not calculated in the existing article.', '''
<p>The earlier on-time rate is 61 ÷ 70 completed jobs = 87.14%. It can look reassuring while work that was due this week remains unfinished. Add a second card based on the <em>due-date cohort</em>, with the same cutoff and an explicit completion rule.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Separate illustrative due-date cohort of 80 jobs</caption><thead><tr><th>At the weekly cutoff</th><th>Jobs</th><th>Treatment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Completed on or before promise</td><td>61</td><td>On-time numerator</td></tr><tr><td>Completed after promise</td><td>9</td><td>Late, although finished</td></tr><tr><td>Still open after promise</td><td>10</td><td>Overdue and unfinished</td></tr><tr><td>Total due in the period</td><td>80</td><td>61 + 9 + 10</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Due-cohort on-time performance is 61 ÷ 80 = <strong>76.25%</strong>. Nineteen jobs missed their promise: nine finished late and ten remain open. This is a separate cohort example; do not add its ten overdue jobs to the earlier stock bridge without reconciling IDs and dates.</p>
<p>Give every exception a job ID, original promised date, reason, next action, owner and revised customer communication. Preserve the original promise when it changes so the history does not become artificially on time. The <a href="/journal/agency-capacity-planning-example/">capacity example</a> helps test whether the recovery work fits; the <a href="/journal/project-management-dashboard/">project dashboard</a> is more suitable when the recovery is a finite project with defined milestones.</p>''')

add('store-performance-dashboard', 'Explain sales growth with traffic, conversion and basket value',
    'Add a reconciled retail sales-driver bridge without confusing fulfilled orders with visitor conversion.', '''
<p>Keep visitor conversion separate from the fulfilled-order cohort above. In this new fictional two-week example, a transaction means a completed in-store sale and visits come from the same counting method in both weeks. No refunds, tax or online orders are included in this particular comparison.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Separate two-week retail driver example</caption><thead><tr><th>Driver</th><th>Week A</th><th>Week B</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Eligible visits</td><td>1,000</td><td>1,200</td></tr><tr><td>Visit-to-transaction conversion</td><td>20%</td><td>18%</td></tr><tr><td>Transactions</td><td>200</td><td>216</td></tr><tr><td>Average basket</td><td>$50</td><td>$50</td></tr><tr><td>Sales</td><td>$10,000</td><td>$10,800</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Sales rose $800, but conversion fell. Using a traffic-first bridge, 200 additional visits at the old 20% conversion and $50 basket add $2,000. Applying the two-percentage-point conversion fall to 1,200 visits subtracts $1,200. Together they explain the $800 increase; no unexplained residual remains.</p>
<p>Investigate stock availability, service coverage and the visit counter before treating higher sales as proof that every process improved. Keep percentage-point changes distinct from percentage changes. Use the <a href="/journal/how-much-inventory-start-boutique/">boutique stock example</a> for size-level availability and the <a href="/tools/reserved-vs-available-stock-planner/">reserved versus available stock worksheet</a> when an apparent stock balance cannot support the next order. Compare stores only after checking opening hours, channel mix and measurement definitions.</p>''')

add('nonprofit-business-plan', 'Write a funding contingency before promising the next program month',
    'Add a practical continuity decision using unrestricted cash and funded delivery capacity.', '''
<p>The reduced 100-appointment scenario needs $8,100 of listed monthly spending and leaves $5,900 unrestricted cash after the example month. If no new unrestricted receipts arrive, that balance covers only $5,900 ÷ $8,100 = <strong>0.73 months</strong> at the same spending pace. This is a rough planning ratio; exact bill dates and restrictions still control the decision.</p>
<p>Do not add the separate $6,000 restricted grant or the uncollected $3,000 of pledges to that spending balance. A probability-weighted grant forecast also cannot pay a confirmed bill. Keep an awarded-but-uncollected amount separate from a cash receipt and preserve its permitted-use terms.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Copyable program continuity decisions</caption><thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Evidence to retain</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Continue the next intake</td><td>Eligible cash by payment date and confirmed volunteer/service coverage</td></tr><tr><td>Reduce or defer activity</td><td>People affected, safe transition arrangements and the authorized decision</td></tr><tr><td>Seek replacement funding</td><td>Fund purpose, application owner, decision date and collection evidence</td></tr><tr><td>Review outcomes</td><td>Defined outcome, follow-up method, consent and limitations; not only appointments counted</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Put these decisions into the <a href="/tools/business-plan-template/">written operating plan</a> and use the <a href="/journal/cash-flow-forecast-template-example/">dated cash forecast</a> to test the earliest shortage. Document who can approve a change and who informs participants. A lower cost per appointment does not, by itself, demonstrate better outcomes or safe service delivery.</p>''')

add('planner-sizes-chart-printable-inserts', 'Calculate a scale change before printing incompatible inserts',
    'Add a numeric print-scaling example with physical verification and binding limits.', '''
<p>If an A5 design must fit a Half Letter sheet, calculate both directions before choosing a print percentage. A5 is 148 × 210 mm; Half Letter is 139.7 × 215.9 mm. The width ratio is 139.7 ÷ 148 = 94.39%, while the height ratio is 215.9 ÷ 210 = 102.81%. Width is the limiting direction.</p>
<p>At 94.39% scale the A5 design becomes approximately 139.7 × 198.2 mm, before printer margins. It fits the sheet geometrically, but it is now a smaller design with smaller handwriting lines. It is <strong>not an actual-size A5 insert</strong>, and its original hole positions and cut marks are no longer reliable.</p>
<ol><li>Check whether the creator supplies the exact insert size first.</li><li>If rescaling is permitted and useful, leave additional room for the printer's nonprintable area and binding margin.</li><li>Print one page, measure its reference line and compare it with an existing insert.</li><li>Check turning clearance and dividers before printing the remaining pages.</li></ol>
<p>The <a href="/journal/a5-planner-sizes-layouts-digital-options/">A5 layout guide</a> helps assess writing space; the <a href="/tools/printable-planner-builder/">printable planner builder</a> offers a separate way to prepare a supported page. Screen zoom is not a substitute for a measured paper test. Keep the original download unchanged so a trial scale does not overwrite your master file.</p>''')

add('2026-monthly-planner-free-layouts', 'Check the November-to-December handoff in the dated PDF',
    'Add a current-year date validation and practical year-end handoff instead of another generic format comparison.', '''
<p>Starting late in the year is valid: use the remaining pages rather than copying ten months of old tasks. For the dated 2026 download, November 1 falls on Sunday and December 1 falls on Tuesday. On a Sunday-first grid, November has 30 dates and December has 31. Confirm the printed year and weekday alignment before entering real appointments.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>A year-end handoff you can copy</caption><thead><tr><th>Record</th><th>November action</th><th>December or next-year action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Confirmed appointment</td><td>Enter the actual date and source</td><td>Carry forward only if it really repeats</td></tr><tr><td>Unfinished project</td><td>Record the next action and remaining effort</td><td>Reserve a feasible work block</td></tr><tr><td>Future commitment outside the file</td><td>Keep a clearly labeled future-date list</td><td>Transfer to the correct dated year</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Do not relabel a 2026 page as 2027: the weekday alignment changes. This file remains a 2026 resource, not an implied next-year update. Use the <a href="/journal/monthly-planner-free-templates/">undated monthly sheet</a> for a month outside its range, or the <a href="/journal/planner-2026-what-to-look-for/">dated-planner purchase checklist</a> before buying another annual file. Verify holidays and school closures with their actual issuer; a blank calendar is not a holiday authority.</p>''')

add('a5-planner-sizes-layouts-digital-options', 'Budget the writing area, not just the A5 sheet',
    'Add a measured usable-area example for choosing between daily and weekly A5 layouts.', '''
<p>Two A5 layouts can have identical outside dimensions and very different usable space. In a separate design example, reserve 12 mm at the binding edge, 8 mm outside, and 10 mm at the top and bottom. The 148 × 210 mm sheet then has a <strong>128 × 190 mm</strong> writing rectangle before headings.</p>
<p>If a daily timeline needs 16 rows at an assumed 8 mm each, the rows use 128 mm of height. A 20 mm heading leaves 42 mm for notes within that 190 mm rectangle. A weekly design that divides the same 128 mm width into seven equal columns gives only 18.29 mm per day before gutters. That may be too narrow for a full handwritten appointment title.</p>
<p>These are illustrative design allowances, not a rule for every binder or handwriting style. Print a sample and write a long appointment, a task with a deadline and a correction using the pen you intend to carry. Test whether the binding hand can reach the inner column comfortably.</p>
<p>Use the <a href="/journal/planner-sizes-chart-printable-inserts/">full insert-size chart</a> when the physical size is uncertain. If the sheet fits but the week feels crowded, compare the <a href="/journal/day-planner-vs-daily-planner/">daily layout options</a> rather than shrinking the type until the page becomes difficult to use. An A5-shaped PDF on a tablet can be zoomed; a printed insert cannot.</p>''')

add('agency-capacity-planning-example', 'Keep weighted pipeline hours outside the signed-work promise',
    'Extend signed capacity planning with an explicit probabilistic pipeline versus full-win stress test.', '''
<p>The existing launch test uses a proposed 30 development hours. If that opportunity is assigned a fictional 40% probability, its weighted forecast is 12 hours. Adding 12 to Leo's 115 signed hours gives 127 forecast hours against 128 usable hours. The weighted view appears to fit by one hour.</p>
<p>But if the client signs, all 30 hours arrive: demand becomes 145 hours and the actual gap is 17. You cannot promise a delivery date on the assumption that only 40% of a signed project will need doing. Keep three columns: signed remaining work, weighted unsigned demand and full-win demand by role and week.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Same opportunity, different decisions</caption><thead><tr><th>View</th><th>Development hours</th><th>Decision supported</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Signed work only</td><td>115</td><td>Current delivery commitments</td></tr><tr><td>Signed plus weighted prospect</td><td>127</td><td>Aggregate hiring or pipeline discussion</td></tr><tr><td>Signed plus full prospect</td><td>145</td><td>Whether this exact start date can be accepted</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Review probabilities against your own past stage outcomes; a sales label is not evidence that a percentage is calibrated. For the current proposal, compare scope, dates and reviewed contractor capacity before agreement. The <a href="/journal/consulting-business-plan/">consulting plan</a> connects the sales funnel to delivery, while the <a href="/tools/freelancer-weekly-capacity-planner/">weekly capacity worksheet</a> helps test the near-term work that is actually due.</p>''')

add('batch-lot-traceability-log', 'Trace a split shipment without duplicating the affected quantity',
    'Add a split-shipment event test beyond the existing input-lot and finished-lot examples.', '''
<p>A lot can appear on several shipment lines without becoming several lots. In this separate fictional dispatch example, finished lot FL-1003 sends 12 units to customer A, eight to customer B and five more to customer A on a later shipment. Each shipment needs its own reference, date, customer reference and quantity.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Split-shipment trace test</caption><thead><tr><th>Shipment</th><th>Customer</th><th>Lot</th><th>Units</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>SHIP-01</td><td>A</td><td>FL-1003</td><td>12</td></tr><tr><td>SHIP-02</td><td>B</td><td>FL-1003</td><td>8</td></tr><tr><td>SHIP-03</td><td>A</td><td>FL-1003</td><td>5</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>The trace result is <strong>25 dispatched units, three shipments, two distinct customer references and one finished lot</strong>. Counting customer A once is correct for a recipient list, but dropping its second shipment would understate units. Conversely, joining each shipment to two input-material rows and summing the repeated shipment quantities would incorrectly produce 50 units.</p>
<p>Aggregate unique movement IDs before joining material genealogy. Keep returned or quarantined units as later events linked to the original movement, not silent edits to its history. Use the <a href="/journal/purchase-order-tracker/">purchase-order receipt example</a> for the inbound document relationship and the <a href="/journal/equipment-calibration-log/">equipment event-log guide</a> when a production investigation needs instrument evidence. This is a record-design exercise, not an industry-specific recall procedure or certification.</p>''')

add('best-2026-planners-digital-printable-options', 'Use a pass/fail trial before comparing decorative extras',
    'Add concrete format acceptance criteria while preserving this URL as a format comparison, not an invented product ranking.', '''
<p>Choose the format with the fewest failures on your normal week. A list of page counts or stickers cannot show whether you can record a changed appointment, find tomorrow's work or recover a lost working copy. Use the same fictional entries in each candidate so the comparison stays fair.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>One-week format trial</caption><thead><tr><th>Test</th><th>Pass evidence</th><th>Reason to reject or investigate</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Change a timed commitment</td><td>Old and new time are understandable</td><td>Two conflicting appointments remain</td></tr><tr><td>Move unfinished work</td><td>One next action has a feasible new slot</td><td>Tasks accumulate without time</td></tr><tr><td>Find a past decision</td><td>You locate the note and its date</td><td>The information cannot be found reliably</td></tr><tr><td>Recover your work</td><td>A spare copy restores the needed record</td><td>Only the untouched original file exists</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Record a short reason for each failure instead of combining incomparable features into an arbitrary score. A paper notebook can pass a quick-access test and fail a searchable-archive requirement; a complex app can do the reverse. Keep the requirement that matters to you explicit.</p>
<p>For the time horizon, use the <a href="/journal/planners-2026-full-comparison/">daily, weekly and monthly comparison</a>. For price, year coverage and access terms, use the <a href="/journal/planner-2026-what-to-look-for/">buying checklist</a>. This page compares formats; it does not certify particular sellers or claim that one brand is universally best.</p>''')

add('best-digital-planner-for-ipad', 'Separate a planner file test from an iPad app test',
    'Add an explicit selection rubric distinguishing missing PDF features from unsupported app features.', '''
<p>When a planner fails a trial, identify which layer failed before replacing it. A missing tab is usually a file-design question; an import error is an app or file-format question. A PDF cannot gain reminder notifications merely because its artwork looks like an app dashboard.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>iPad planner decision record</caption><thead><tr><th>Your requirement</th><th>What to inspect</th><th>Useful trial</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Handwritten planning</td><td>Writing area in the actual PDF and annotation tools in the app</td><td>Write a long task without constant zoom changes</td></tr><tr><td>Navigation</td><td>Links actually embedded in the file</td><td>Open a month, a week and a day; return to the index</td></tr><tr><td>Reminders</td><td>A documented task/calendar function</td><td>Create a harmless test alert in the supplying app</td></tr><tr><td>Recovery</td><td>Working-copy backup, not just the purchased download</td><td>Open a separate restored test copy</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Mark a requirement unconfirmed when the seller supplies only cover images. Ask for the relevant specification or use a sample; do not infer capabilities from appearance. Check device compatibility through <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/108937" rel="noopener">Apple's current Pencil compatibility list</a> rather than assuming every stylus works with every iPad.</p>
<p>Use the <a href="/journal/ipad-digital-planner-setup-guide/">PDF import walkthrough</a> after choosing a file. If the decision is between task managers and annotation apps, switch to the <a href="/journal/best-planner-app-for-ipad-2026/">app comparison</a>; buying a different PDF will not resolve a requirement for native alerts or a shared live database.</p>''')

add('best-planner-app-for-ipad-2026', 'Choose a single owner for each kind of record',
    'Add a concrete app-selection workflow and eliminate ambiguous claims that every planner must do everything.', '''
<p>A useful iPad setup can use more than one app, provided each record has one authoritative home. Duplicating the same due date in three apps creates maintenance work, even when every individual app is capable. Decide where changes begin and which other pages are only summaries.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Example ownership map, not a product ranking</caption><thead><tr><th>Record</th><th>Authoritative home</th><th>Planner-page treatment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Appointment and time alert</td><td>Your chosen calendar</td><td>Reference the commitment; verify changes against the calendar</td></tr><tr><td>Action requiring a reminder</td><td>Your chosen task list</td><td>Write today's next step, without a second competing due date</td></tr><tr><td>Handwritten session notes</td><td>Your annotation notebook</td><td>Date the page and link or name the related task</td></tr><tr><td>Project source documents</td><td>Your chosen document store</td><td>Keep a reference, not a confidential duplicate in every planner</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Try a changed class deadline: update the authoritative source, revise the affected work blocks and check every summary you deliberately maintain. If that takes several manual corrections, simplify the setup before moving a full semester. This is a workflow test, not evidence that the apps automatically synchronize with one another.</p>
<p>The <a href="/journal/student-planner-guide/">student planning guide</a> shows how to turn the deadline into available study time. The <a href="/journal/ipad-planner-app-buying-guide/">purchase checklist</a> handles current subscription terms, compatibility and backup evidence separately. Prefer a documented feature to a broad claim that an app is an all-in-one planner.</p>''')

add('business-plan-financial-projections-service-example', 'Attach a balance-sheet bridge when the reader needs full projections',
    'Clarify the tool boundary and add an independent receivables/cash reconciliation for a full financial narrative.', '''
<p>The worksheet models operating results and cash movements; it does not generate a balance sheet. A lender or investor may ask for a wider set of statements. Explain that boundary rather than presenting a two-month cash table as a complete financial package. The <a href="https://www.sba.gov/business-guide/plan-your-business/write-your-business-plan" rel="noopener">SBA business-plan guidance</a> includes projected statements and a clearly explained first-year outlook.</p>
<p>For the October example, suppose opening customer receivables are zero, all $6,000 of billed revenue belongs to this month, and $5,000 is collected against those invoices. Closing receivables are $0 + $6,000 − $5,000 = <strong>$1,000</strong>, before credits, write-offs or other adjustments. This separate simplified bridge explains where the uncollected revenue sits; it does not add $1,000 to bank cash.</p>
<p>In the $1,000 collection-delay scenario, October collections become $4,000 and receivables become $2,000 on the same assumptions. Closing cash falls by $1,000 while operating revenue is unchanged. The additional receivable and the cash shortfall are two sides of the timing change, not two independent losses.</p>
<p>Prepare an evidence list for opening assets, obligations, tax assumptions, depreciation and funding before attempting the full statement set. Use the <a href="/journal/cash-flow-forecast-template-example/">weekly cash forecast</a> for dates within the month and the <a href="/journal/consulting-business-plan/">consulting operating plan</a> to validate the work behind revenue. Retain the original forecast when creating a downside version.</p>''')

add('cash-flow-forecast-template-example', 'Reconcile bank accounts and internal transfers before rolling forward',
    'Add a cash-consolidation example so internal transfers do not inflate forecast receipts.', '''
<p>If the business uses two included bank accounts, moving money between them is not new cash for the consolidated forecast. In this independent fictional example, operating account A starts at $3,000 and reserve account B at $2,000. A $1,000 transfer from B to A leaves $4,000 in A and $1,000 in B; total included cash remains <strong>$5,000</strong>.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Consolidated weekly cash bridge</caption><thead><tr><th>Movement</th><th>Amount</th><th>Consolidated treatment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Opening A plus B</td><td>$5,000</td><td>Included starting cash</td></tr><tr><td>Internal B-to-A transfer</td><td>$1,000</td><td>Net zero; account movements offset</td></tr><tr><td>Customer receipts</td><td>+$2,400</td><td>External inflow</td></tr><tr><td>Supplier and other payments</td><td>−$3,100</td><td>External outflow</td></tr><tr><td>Closing included cash</td><td>$4,300</td><td>$5,000 + $2,400 − $3,100</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Identify whether a reserve account is included and whether any balance is restricted. If this is an operating-account-only report, the transfer appears in that account's schedule, but label the narrower boundary. Do not mix an all-bank opening balance with operating-account-only movements.</p>
<p>Before adding week 14, reconcile bank balances, unresolved payments and receipts already counted. The <a href="/journal/small-business-budget-example/">budget example</a> explains why expenses and cash differ; the <a href="/journal/business-plan-financial-projections-service-example/">financial projections example</a> connects monthly collections to billed revenue. Keep the forecast basis next to the opening balance so a later reviewer can reproduce it.</p>''')

add('coffee-shop-business-plan-daily-capacity', 'Test the peak half-hour, not just the daily average',
    'Extend the daily capacity model with a reconciled peak service bottleneck and a queue response.', '''
<p>The 120-order day averages 15 orders per hour across eight hours, but an average can hide a queue the shop cannot serve. Suppose a separate trial expects 18 orders in the busiest half-hour and the tested service process can complete only 12 in that interval. The backlog grows by <strong>six orders</strong>, even if the rest of the day is quiet.</p>
<p>If arrivals then slow to eight orders per half-hour while capacity stays at 12, the shop can clear four backlog orders in the next interval. Two remain at its end. This is a simple count model: it ignores different drink preparation times, batching and abandoned queues, so it cannot establish a promised waiting time.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Separate two-interval peak test</caption><thead><tr><th>Interval</th><th>Opening backlog</th><th>Arrivals</th><th>Completed</th><th>Closing backlog</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Peak half-hour</td><td>0</td><td>18</td><td>12</td><td>6</td></tr><tr><td>Following half-hour</td><td>6</td><td>8</td><td>12</td><td>2</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Before buying more equipment, observe which step constrains service: ordering, payment, preparation or handoff. Test a bounded change and record labor, waste and customer response. An extra staff member helps only if the bottleneck can use that person's work.</p>
<p>Put the trial evidence in the <a href="/tools/business-plan-template/">business plan narrative</a> and track queue movements using the <a href="/journal/operations-dashboard-example/">operations dashboard example</a>. Recalculate the daily cost model when staffing changes; peak coverage is not free simply because the average order target is unchanged.</p>''')

add('construction-company-business-plan-milestone-cash', 'Show retention separately from the milestone invoice',
    'Add a bounded retention sensitivity without claiming universal construction payment terms.', '''
<p>A milestone amount can differ from the cash expected at that milestone. In this separate fictional sensitivity, a $3,000 approved invoice has an agreed 10% retention. The near-term expected receipt is $2,700 and the retained $300 needs its own condition and expected release date. These are invented terms, not a rule for construction contracts.</p>
<p>If the existing cash schedule expected all $3,000 on October 23, replacing that receipt with $2,700 lowers every later balance by $300 until the retention is actually collected. A $200 closing balance would become −$100. Releasing the retention in a later week moves cash; it does not create a second $300 of project revenue.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Milestone collection record</caption><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Fictional entry</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Approved invoice</td><td>$3,000, with the approval evidence reference</td></tr><tr><td>Retention assumption</td><td>10%, or $300</td></tr><tr><td>Near-term receipt</td><td>$2,700 on the currently supported date</td></tr><tr><td>Later receipt</td><td>$300 only when the actual release condition and timing are supported</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Keep disputed work, unapproved changes and retention distinct. Verify the actual contract and applicable requirements before using any payment assumption. Use the <a href="/journal/cash-flow-forecast-template-example/">company cash forecast</a> to consolidate concurrent jobs and the <a href="/tools/business-startup-checklist/">startup checklist</a> to document missing operating evidence. Do not repair a negative balance by silently removing an obligation or allocating the same bank cash to several projects.</p>''')

add('daily-planner-complete-guide', 'Replan from remaining time when the morning slips',
    'Add an explicit recovery calculation instead of another ideal-day timetable.', '''
<p>A daily plan needs a recovery rule as well as a morning setup. In a separate example, it is 1:00 p.m. and work must stop at 5:00 p.m. The four remaining hours contain a 60-minute meeting, 30 minutes of travel and a 15-minute break, leaving <strong>135 minutes</strong> before any new discretionary work.</p>
<p>Your remaining tasks need 90, 60 and 30 minutes: 180 minutes altogether. The plan is 45 minutes over capacity. Moving every task into the afternoon does not solve the problem. Choose a smaller deliverable, move one task with an explicit consequence, or seek a change to a commitment that can genuinely move.</p>
<ol><li>Write the actual cutoff time and commitments that remain.</li><li>Estimate only the unfinished portion of each task.</li><li>Put the most time-sensitive feasible action into a real slot.</li><li>Record what moved, its new date and who must be informed.</li></ol>
<p>In this example, completing the 90-minute task and the 30-minute task uses 120 minutes and leaves a 15-minute margin; the 60-minute task needs another agreed slot. The margin is not a universal buffer recommendation.</p>
<p>Use the <a href="/tools/time-blocking-template/">time-blocking worksheet</a> to lay out the revised afternoon and the <a href="/journal/hourly-planner-best-free-digital-layouts/">hourly planning guide</a> to check boundaries and overlapping entries. Keep the earlier plan available for review so repeated overruns can improve future estimates.</p>''')

add('day-planner-vs-daily-planner', 'Compare the inside page rather than the product name',
    'Clarify synonymous naming and add a layout selection test without duplicating the full daily-planning workflow.', '''
<p>“Day planner” and “daily planner” are commonly overlapping labels, not two standardized product specifications. One seller may use either term for a page per day; another may sell a weekly book under the same name. The inside-page preview is stronger evidence than the cover title.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>What to compare when the names are ambiguous</caption><thead><tr><th>Visible page feature</th><th>Useful when</th><th>Check before choosing</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Timed daily column</td><td>Appointments, travel and hard cutoffs control the day</td><td>The printed hours cover your actual day</td></tr><tr><td>Open daily task page</td><td>A few outcomes matter more than an hourly grid</td><td>Tasks still have a realistic time allowance</td></tr><tr><td>Weekly spread in a “day planner”</td><td>You mostly need to compare days</td><td>Each day has enough writing space</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Try the same changed appointment and unfinished task on each sample. If the wording is short but the page still becomes crowded, the problem is layout density rather than whether the seller says “day” or “daily.” A separate notes page may help more than another branded system.</p>
<p>For a usable blank page and a recovery routine, open the <a href="/journal/daily-planner-complete-guide/">daily planner template guide</a>. For the bigger choice between one day, one week and one month at a time, use the <a href="/journal/planners-2026-full-comparison/">planning-horizon comparison</a>. Keep this naming decision separate from file type, device requirements and saving behavior.</p>''')

add('digital-planner-app-how-it-works', 'Follow one entry from creation to a recoverable copy',
    'Explain the data lifecycle across formats rather than equating digital planning with a PDF.', '''
<p>A digital planner works through a chain: create an entry, store it, reopen it, change it and recover it if the working copy is lost. The chain differs by format. Testing only whether the original file opens misses the part that protects your later work.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Trace a fictional “review proposal” entry</caption><thead><tr><th>Format</th><th>Where the entry belongs</th><th>What to test independently</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Annotated PDF</td><td>The annotation app's working document</td><td>Reopen the annotation and restore a spare document copy</td></tr><tr><td>Cloud workspace</td><td>The signed-in workspace and its documented offline copy</td><td>Confirm the entry on the intended device and inspect export limits</td></tr><tr><td>Local HTML worksheet</td><td>The app's documented storage for that browser/file workflow</td><td>Export and safely restore its editable backup</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>A screenshot proves that an entry was visible, not that it is recoverable. A CSV may preserve rows without restoring application settings or relationships. The untouched purchased download contains the original design, not the notes added afterward. Name both the working location and backup location before entering important records.</p>
<p>Use the <a href="/journal/ipad-digital-planner-setup-guide/">iPad PDF walkthrough</a> for the annotation route. Use the <a href="/journal/inventory-software-without-subscription/">local-software buying tests</a> when the record is operational stock rather than a handwritten schedule. Only rely on reminders, collaboration or sync when the selected app actually documents and passes the feature you need.</p>''')

add('digital-planner-for-ipad-setup', 'Use a format decision tree before following setup instructions',
    'Separate format triage from the dedicated PDF installation URL and add a useful troubleshooting matrix.', '''
<p>Start with the file you actually received. A ZIP is a container: inspect the extracted contents before choosing an app. A PDF, a workspace invitation and an HTML application need different setup paths. Renaming an extension does not convert one into another.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Choose the next setup action</caption><thead><tr><th>What you received</th><th>Next action</th><th>Stop and check if</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A planner PDF</td><td>Import a working copy into a compatible annotation app</td><td>The file opens as an unexpected blank or incomplete document</td></tr><tr><td>A workspace/template link</td><td>Follow that provider's duplication and account instructions</td><td>You cannot identify which account owns the resulting copy</td></tr><tr><td>An HTML app and instructions</td><td>Verify the stated iPad file-opening and storage workflow</td><td>It opens only as a noninteractive preview or cannot save the test entry</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>For every route, make one fictional entry, close the working copy and reopen it from the location you will use tomorrow. Then test a spare backup. A successful desktop demonstration is not proof that the same downloaded HTML file works in an iPad previewer.</p>
<p>Continue to the <a href="/journal/ipad-digital-planner-setup-guide/">step-by-step PDF installation guide</a> when PDF is the actual format. If the file is not yet chosen, use the <a href="/journal/best-digital-planner-for-ipad/">iPad planner format comparison</a>. This page handles the route decision; the dedicated PDF page handles the import procedure.</p>''')

add('equipment-calibration-log', 'Record the interval decision and the acceptance decision separately',
    'Add authoritative interval-setting limits and a useful evidence register without promising compliance.', '''
<p>A due-date calculation cannot establish that a calibration result is suitable for the instrument's intended work. Keep two decisions: the documented interval rule used for scheduling, and the review of the returned measurement evidence against your actual acceptance requirements.</p>
<p><a href="https://www.nist.gov/calibrations/recommended-calibration-interval" rel="noopener">NIST does not prescribe one general recalibration interval</a>. Its guidance identifies factors such as required accuracy, external requirements, device stability and environment. A 180-day example is therefore a documented assumption, not a universal NIST rule.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Evidence for the reviewer</caption><thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Record</th><th>Unresolved condition</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>When is it due?</td><td>Approved rule, basis, service date and calculation</td><td>Missing interval basis or conflicting dates</td></tr><tr><td>May it return to the intended use?</td><td>Instrument identity, relevant results, uncertainty, applicable acceptance rule and reviewer</td><td>Missing evidence or a result needing investigation</td></tr><tr><td>Was prior work affected?</td><td>Use history, affected work references and authorized disposition</td><td>Impact review remains open</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>The <a href="https://www.nist.gov/metrology/metrological-traceability" rel="noopener">NIST traceability guidance</a> concerns supported measurement-result claims; a certificate number alone is not the whole claim. Use the <a href="/journal/batch-lot-traceability-log/">batch event relationships</a> to locate potentially affected production records, and the <a href="/tools/equipment-maintenance-due-planner/">maintenance-due worksheet</a> only for its stated scheduling purpose. Neither a green date cell nor this article certifies fitness, accreditation or regulatory compliance.</p>''')

add('equipment-rental-business-plan-example', 'Keep physical-calendar utilization beside available-date utilization',
    'Add a reconciled maintenance denominator sensitivity and preserve the distinction between rate improvement and sales growth.', '''
<p>The original kit has 20 potentially rentable dates and ten bookings, giving 50% utilization. In a separate maintenance sensitivity, five of those dates become unavailable. The kit still completes ten bookings, but availability-adjusted utilization becomes 10 ÷ 15 = <strong>66.67%</strong>.</p>
<p>Gross receipts remain $900 at the same $90 booking price, and the original $18-per-booking variable costs remain $180. The percentage improved because the denominator fell; no additional customer was served. Record the repair payment and any lost requests separately before interpreting the change as better performance.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Two utilization views for the same ten bookings</caption><thead><tr><th>View</th><th>Denominator</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Original planned date capacity</td><td>20 dates</td><td>50%</td></tr><tr><td>Available dates after closure</td><td>15 dates</td><td>66.67%</td></tr><tr><td>Revenue at unchanged booking count</td><td>10 × $90</td><td>$900 in both views</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Distinguish reservations, completed hires, blocked turnaround dates and maintenance closures in the asset record. A cancelled booking should release availability only under the actual status and turnaround rules. Use the <a href="/journal/operations-dashboard-example/">operations dashboard</a> to keep those movements visible and the <a href="/tools/equipment-maintenance-due-planner/">maintenance planning worksheet</a> to record service assumptions. Add another kit only after checking peak-date conflicts, contribution and the cash needed for the purchase.</p>''')

add('free-digital-planner-for-ipad', 'Know exactly what the free file includes before importing it',
    'Make the no-cost deliverable scope explicit and add an evidence-based free-resource comparison.', '''
<p>The free file linked on this page is a static daily planning PDF. It supplies a worksheet; it is not a complete linked yearly planner, a native iPad app, a subscription entitlement or an automatic reminder service. Your chosen annotation app may have its own current charges and limits.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Check the offer, not only the word “free”</caption><thead><tr><th>Offer detail</th><th>Evidence to inspect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Download scope</td><td>Actual file type, page count and dated or undated coverage</td></tr><tr><td>Access condition</td><td>Immediate download, email requirement, account or trial expiry</td></tr><tr><td>App requirement</td><td>Supported import format and the app's own current terms</td></tr><tr><td>Reuse permission</td><td>Personal, classroom or business permission for the specific file</td></tr><tr><td>Recovery</td><td>A saved original plus a recoverable copy of later annotations</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Try one normal day before collecting more pages. Record a fixed appointment, one important task and a restart note. Reopen the working document and check that the annotation remains. Then keep a spare readable or editable copy appropriate to the app; downloading the blank PDF again will not recover your notes.</p>
<p>For a different no-cost page, use the <a href="/journal/monthly-planner-free-templates/">undated monthly PDF</a>. For the actual import sequence, use the <a href="/journal/ipad-digital-planner-setup-guide/">PDF setup guide</a>. Paid NexalyPlanner products remain separate offers with their own file formats and requirements.</p>''')

add('freelance-business-plan-example', 'Test the loss of one client before treating bookings as stable income',
    'Add client concentration sensitivity using the existing package fee with an explicitly defined cost split.', '''
<p>Three $1,250 packages produce $3,750 of invoiced work, but each package represents one-third of that total. Test a cancellation or a missing renewal before assuming the same amount will recur. In this separate sensitivity, treat the earlier $180 software cost as fixed for the month and the $420 contractor cost as $140 per package.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Separate client-concentration test</caption><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Three packages</th><th>Two packages</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Invoiced revenue</td><td>$3,750</td><td>$2,500</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>$180</td><td>$180</td></tr><tr><td>Contractor cost</td><td>$420</td><td>$280</td></tr><tr><td>Before other overhead, owner pay and tax</td><td>$3,150</td><td>$2,040</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>The lost package reduces the remainder by $1,110, not $1,250, because $140 of assumed variable contractor cost is avoided. If the contractor fee is already committed, it cannot simply disappear from the downside case. Confirm the real terms before changing a cost.</p>
<p>Write a response that fits the actual available hours: follow up a qualified opportunity, offer a bounded smaller scope or reduce a discretionary outlay. Do not replace lost work with an unsupported sales target. The <a href="/journal/consulting-business-plan/">consulting funnel example</a> helps test acquisition assumptions, and the <a href="/journal/cash-flow-forecast-template-example/">cash forecast</a> shows whether a late payment creates a shortage before replacement work can be collected.</p>''')

add('hourly-planner-best-free-digital-layouts', 'Distinguish task duration from the slots you reserve',
    'Add a useful rounding example for half-hour grids without overstating actual work duration.', '''
<p>A half-hour grid can create hidden reservations when each task is rounded up independently. Four tasks estimated at 45 minutes each need 180 minutes of actual work. If each task must occupy whole 30-minute slots, each reserves 60 minutes, or <strong>240 minutes</strong> altogether. The extra 60 minutes is reserved space, not proof that the tasks themselves require four hours.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Separate half-hour grid example</caption><thead><tr><th>Measure</th><th>Calculation</th><th>Minutes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Estimated work</td><td>4 × 45</td><td>180</td></tr><tr><td>Whole-slot reservations</td><td>4 × 2 × 30</td><td>240</td></tr><tr><td>Unallocated space inside reservations</td><td>240 − 180</td><td>60</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Choose whether those gaps are deliberate transition buffers, usable adjacent work time or simply an overly coarse layout. Do not schedule a second overlapping task into a gap that is already reserved for travel, cleanup or a required handoff. If tasks can move flexibly, a finer grid or an untimed list beside fixed appointments may fit better.</p>
<p>Use the <a href="/tools/time-blocking-template/">time-blocking worksheet</a> to compare the actual start and end times. The <a href="/journal/daily-planner-complete-guide/">daily recovery example</a> helps when delays consume the afternoon. Preserve the real duration estimate separately from the calendar reservation so next week's review does not confuse extra buffer with slow work.</p>''')

add('inventory-software-without-subscription', 'Test an exit migration before depending on the inventory record',
    'Add a data-portability acceptance exercise beyond price and simple backup checks.', '''
<p>A one-time license does not answer what happens when you replace the tool or computer. Before using it as the stock record, run a small migration exercise with fictional SKUs. Inspect both the exported fields and the destination's interpretation; an export button alone is not enough.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Migration acceptance record</caption><thead><tr><th>Test item</th><th>Evidence to compare</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Identity</td><td>SKU and variant IDs remain distinct; leading zeros do not vanish</td></tr><tr><td>Units</td><td>Cases and eaches retain their unit and conversion meaning</td></tr><tr><td>Availability</td><td>On-hand, reserved and quarantined stock do not collapse into one count</td></tr><tr><td>Value</td><td>Currency, cost basis and decimal precision remain explicit</td></tr><tr><td>History</td><td>Movements retain references and dates, or the missing scope is documented</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>For example, start with 12 on hand, three reserved and two quarantined, assuming both groups are part of the 12 and do not overlap. Available stock is seven. An importer that treats 12 as freely sellable has created a five-unit overstatement even though its total matches the original on-hand column.</p>
<p>Use the <a href="/tools/reserved-vs-available-stock-planner/">availability worksheet</a> to check that definition and the <a href="/journal/purchase-order-tracker/">purchase-order register</a> to distinguish outstanding receipts from stock already received. Keep the original backup and test in a spare environment. A readable CSV may support migration while still being insufficient to restore the original application's complete state.</p>''')

add('ipad-digital-planner-setup-guide', 'Recover the common PDF import and export failures',
    'Add primary-documentation troubleshooting with a restore rehearsal to the short installation guide.', '''
<p>If setup fails, identify the exact stage before downloading another copy. Keep the original file and use a fictional test entry while checking recovery.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>PDF setup troubleshooting</caption><thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Check</th><th>Next action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Wrong or repeated pages</td><td>Whether you imported into an existing document instead of creating the intended new one</td><td>Retain your notes, then follow the documented new-document import route</td></tr><tr><td>Tapping draws instead of navigating</td><td>The app's current link/navigation controls</td><td>Try the documented read-only or link-opening action</td></tr><tr><td>Links worked before export but not afterward</td><td>PDF export format</td><td>Test a fresh editable export where original links must remain</td></tr><tr><td>The blank file is safe but notes are missing</td><td>Whether the working notebook was backed up</td><td>Restore a separate test copy from the appropriate notebook backup</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Goodnotes documents <a href="https://support.goodnotes.com/hc/en-us/articles/7353717816463-Import-files-into-Goodnotes" rel="noopener">new and existing document import routes</a>. Its <a href="https://support.goodnotes.com/hc/en-us/articles/7353742854543-PDF-outlines-and-hyperlinks-are-missing-when-I-export-a-document" rel="noopener">export troubleshooting</a> distinguishes preservation of original PDF links from a flattened output. Check the current app instructions rather than relying on an old toolbar screenshot.</p>
<p>Finish with a harmless restore rehearsal: add a dated test note, make the intended backup, open a separate restored copy and find that note. Do not delete your working notebook to test recovery. For file selection use the <a href="/journal/digital-planner-for-ipad-setup/">format decision guide</a>; for reminders and calendar ownership use the <a href="/journal/best-planner-app-for-ipad-2026/">app workflow comparison</a>.</p>''')

add('ipad-planner-app-buying-guide', 'Compare the full cost of the same usable setup',
    'Add a transparent total-cost example with fictional amounts and no unsupported current vendor prices.', '''
<p>Compare the cost of reaching your required workflow, not only the planner-file price. In this separate fictional three-year comparison, assume you already own a compatible iPad and stylus. Hardware, tax and optional accessories are excluded.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Illustrative costs, not vendor offers</caption><thead><tr><th>Setup</th><th>Assumptions</th><th>Three-year total</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>One purchased PDF plus annual app</td><td>$18 file + $12/year app for 3 years</td><td>$54</td></tr><tr><td>Annual planner file plus annual app</td><td>($18 file + $12 app) × 3 years</td><td>$90</td></tr><tr><td>Included app and no-cost file</td><td>$0 additional file/app charge under the assumed terms</td><td>$0 additional cost</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>The $36 difference between the first two options comes from buying two additional annual files, not from a better handwriting feature. Do not assume a dated file includes next year's edition. Add any actual cloud-storage, renewal or export charges needed for your required setup. A free tier counts only if its limits support the intended use.</p>
<p>Record the offer URL, checking date, renewal basis, what happens after cancellation and the backup format before paying. Use the <a href="/journal/free-digital-planner-for-ipad/">free-file scope guide</a> for a small trial and the <a href="/journal/ipad-digital-planner-setup-guide/">PDF installation guide</a> to test the working copy. Do not buy replacement hardware solely because an attractive preview omits compatibility information.</p>''')

add('monthly-planner-free-templates', 'Place a six-row month without losing its final dates',
    'Add a precise undated-calendar placement example that differs from the dated 2026 resource.', '''
<p>A Sunday-first monthly grid sometimes needs six rows. August 2026 begins on Saturday and has 31 days. Six blank cells precede August 1, so the month occupies 6 + 31 = 37 day positions. Five rows provide only 35 cells; August 30 and 31 need the sixth row.</p>
<p>Do not squeeze both dates into an unlabeled box or erase the weekday alignment. If your printed design has only five rows, use a clearly labeled continuation or choose a suitable six-row layout. The available download's actual grid should guide the placement, not the number of dates visible in a small preview.</p>
<ol><li>Verify the month, year, first weekday and number of days against your calendar.</li><li>Number the full grid before entering commitments.</li><li>Mark any adjacent-month dates as belonging to a different month.</li><li>Check the final week before transferring appointments from another source.</li></ol>
<p>This is why an undated sheet can be reusable without being a prepared calendar for every month. Use the <a href="/journal/2026-monthly-planner-free-layouts/">dated 2026 monthly pages</a> when you want dates already placed. For the work behind an appointment or deadline, use the <a href="/journal/daily-planner-complete-guide/">daily planning page</a> rather than filling a small calendar cell with every task. Keep the confirmed deadline authoritative while scheduling its preparation separately.</p>''')

add('planner-2026-what-to-look-for', 'Check the remaining dated life before a year-end purchase',
    'Add a current-date purchase comparison while keeping 2026 URL/date ownership intact.', '''
<p>On November 10, 2026, a January–December planner has 52 calendar dates left including November 10: 21 in November and 31 in December. That does not mean all 52 will be useful planning days. Count the dates you intend to use and verify whether future-year pages are actually included.</p>
<p>For a purely illustrative $26 file used on every remaining date, the upfront file cost is $26 ÷ 52 = $0.50 per date. If only 26 work or study dates matter, the same file cost is $1 per used date. Neither ratio measures quality, and neither includes a separate app charge or guarantees a refund.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Year-end buying evidence</caption><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Record before purchase</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>What dates are included?</td><td>Actual first and last dates, including any future-year coverage</td></tr><tr><td>Can it be reused?</td><td>Undated pages and documented duplication or reuse permissions</td></tr><tr><td>What renews?</td><td>File editions, app access and storage charges separately</td></tr><tr><td>What happens to notes?</td><td>Readable and restorable copies after access changes</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Use a <a href="/journal/monthly-planner-free-templates/">free undated monthly sheet</a> when the remaining dated coverage does not meet your needs. Compare <a href="/journal/best-2026-planners-digital-printable-options/">paper, printable and digital formats</a> only after checking this coverage. A new cover year does not establish a better planning method or a compatible file.</p>''')

add('planners-2026-full-comparison', 'Choose one authoritative deadline across all three views',
    'Add a concrete change-propagation example to prevent multi-view planning from creating conflicting dates.', '''
<p>Using monthly, weekly and daily views is useful only if each serves a different decision. Keep a confirmed deadline in one authoritative source and treat the other views as planned work, not three competing promises.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Separate example: a report due Friday, November 20, 2026</caption><thead><tr><th>View</th><th>Record</th><th>If the deadline changes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Monthly</td><td>Confirmed November 20 deadline and its source</td><td>Update the authoritative date and note the change</td></tr><tr><td>Weekly</td><td>Research, drafting and review blocks before the deadline</td><td>Recheck their sequence and available hours</td></tr><tr><td>Daily</td><td>One next action with a start/end allowance</td><td>Move only the affected action and its follow-up</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>If the deadline moves to Wednesday, November 18, simply replacing the monthly number leaves the old Thursday review too late. The weekly sequence must change too. If no earlier review block exists, revise scope or agree another solution; do not assume a different layout creates more hours.</p>
<p>Use the <a href="/journal/monthly-planner-free-templates/">monthly sheet</a> for the date overview and the <a href="/journal/hourly-planner-best-free-digital-layouts/">hourly guide</a> to check feasible work blocks. A dated PDF, a paper book and a browser worksheet can all represent these views; automatic propagation between them requires a real documented feature. Start with one view if maintaining three adds more corrections than clarity.</p>''')

add('purchase-order-tracker', 'Track unit conversions before comparing ordered and received quantities',
    'Add a case-to-each receiving example so workbook totals retain a consistent tracking unit.', '''
<p>The order quantity, accepted quantity and cancellation quantity must use the same unit. A separate fictional order is for ten cases of filters, with 12 each per case. The tracking quantity is therefore 120 each. Receiving four complete cases plus six loose filters gives 54 accepted each, not ten or 4.5 accepted units without a label.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Separate case-to-each receipt example</caption><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Calculation</th><th>Each</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ordered</td><td>10 cases × 12</td><td>120</td></tr><tr><td>Accepted</td><td>4 cases × 12 + 6 loose</td><td>54</td></tr><tr><td>Confirmed cancellation</td><td>1 case × 12</td><td>12</td></tr><tr><td>Open</td><td>120 − 54 − 12</td><td>54</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Retain the supplier's order unit and the documented conversion beside the tracking unit. If the case pack changes, do not silently apply the new pack size to earlier receipts. A damaged or rejected unit is not accepted stock just because it arrived physically.</p>
<p>Use the <a href="/tools/purchase-order-template/">purchase-order form</a> for the supplier-facing document and the <a href="/tools/reserved-vs-available-stock-planner/">stock availability worksheet</a> after accepted units enter inventory. The downloadable tracker does not automatically convert mixed units for you; normalize the inputs before entering cumulative quantities. Keep receipt completion, invoice approval and payment status as separate decisions.</p>''')

add('small-business-budget-example', 'Bridge the full $800 shortfall without double-counting volume',
    'Complete the original budget variance reconciliation with a contribution-based bridge.', '''
<p>The earlier budget falls from $2,400 to $1,600 before owner pay, tax and financing: an $800 unfavorable change. Explain that difference with one consistent bridge instead of listing overlapping reasons.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Reconcile the existing monthly budget example</caption><thead><tr><th>Driver</th><th>Calculation</th><th>Effect on remainder</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ten fewer orders at budget contribution</td><td>−10 × ($80 − $28)</td><td>−$520</td></tr><tr><td>Higher unit cost on actual orders</td><td>−90 × ($30 − $28)</td><td>−$180</td></tr><tr><td>Higher fixed costs</td><td>−($2,900 − $2,800)</td><td>−$100</td></tr><tr><td>Total change</td><td>−520 − 180 − 100</td><td>−$800</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>The $520 volume effect already includes both lost sales and the variable costs avoided on the ten missing orders. Subtracting the full $800 sales variance again would count part of the same effect twice. Average price is unchanged at $80 here; add a price or mix step only when the underlying example actually changes it.</p>
<p>Assign one investigation to each material driver: lost orders, the $2 unit-cost increase and the extra $100 fixed spending. Use the <a href="/tools/monthly-business-budget-variance/">budget variance worksheet</a> for the actual period and the <a href="/journal/cash-flow-forecast-template-example/">cash forecast</a> for payment timing. A management variance bridge explains the operating change; it does not establish the cash available for an owner withdrawal.</p>''')

add('student-planner-guide', 'Check dependencies as well as total study hours',
    'Add a backward-planning example where sufficient total hours still fail the submission deadline.', '''
<p>Enough total time does not prove that a study plan is in the right order. In a separate example, an assignment is due Thursday at 4:00 p.m. It needs 60 minutes of source reading, 90 minutes of drafting and 30 minutes of review and submission preparation: <strong>180 minutes</strong> in sequence.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>A plan that adds up but finishes too late</caption><thead><tr><th>Available block</th><th>Minutes</th><th>Initial use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Monday</td><td>90</td><td>Reading, then 30 minutes drafting</td></tr><tr><td>Wednesday</td><td>60</td><td>Finish drafting</td></tr><tr><td>Friday</td><td>30</td><td>Review, after the deadline</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>The arithmetic totals 180 minutes, but only 150 are before submission. Find a real 30-minute review block before Thursday's cutoff or revise the plan with appropriate support. Do not pretend Friday's time is available for a Thursday deadline.</p>
<p>Put the deadline and official submission timezone in the <a href="/tools/assignment-deadline-planner/">assignment deadline worksheet</a>, then put preparation blocks in the <a href="/tools/time-blocking-template/">time-blocking worksheet</a>. Add upload checks and required materials to the final action, and verify changes against the course source. Keep the original estimate and actual work time so later assignments become easier to plan; the planner cannot guarantee a grade or create extra time.</p>''')

add('supplier-scorecard-template', 'Test whether a small weight change reverses the ranking',
    'Add a transparent weight-sensitivity example without changing the downloadable workbook contract.', '''
<p>A weighted total expresses your chosen priorities, not an objective truth about a supplier. Use a separate fictional comparison to see how much the decision depends on weights. Supplier A scores 4, 5, 4 and 3 for delivery, quality, price and response. Supplier B scores 5, 4, 3 and 5 on the same defined scales.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Separate sensitivity; scores stay unchanged</caption><thead><tr><th>Weights: delivery / quality / price / response</th><th>A total</th><th>B total</th><th>Higher total</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>40% / 30% / 20% / 10%</td><td>4.20 / 5</td><td>4.30 / 5</td><td>B</td></tr><tr><td>20% / 50% / 20% / 10%</td><td>4.40 / 5</td><td>4.10 / 5</td><td>A</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>For the first row, A is 4×0.40 + 5×0.30 + 4×0.20 + 3×0.10 = 4.20. The second row shifts 20 percentage points from delivery to quality. The reversal means the tradeoff matters; it is not permission to tune weights after seeing a preferred winner.</p>
<p>Agree the criteria, evidence window, weights and any nonnegotiable gate before scoring. If two suppliers have different inspection coverage, resolve comparability before interpreting a small total difference. Use the <a href="/tools/supplier-lead-time-log/">lead-time log</a> for delivery evidence and the <a href="/journal/purchase-order-tracker/">receipt register</a> to trace individual orders. The existing workbook still requires complete active evidence and weights totaling 100%; it does not automatically make missing evidence comparable.</p>''')

add('trucking-company-business-plan-trip-cost', 'Test the unpaid wait as a monthly capacity constraint',
    'Add a time-based trucking plan sensitivity beyond the existing per-mile and empty-mile calculations.', '''
<p>A load can meet a per-mile threshold and still consume too much of the month's available operating time. In this separate simplified scheduling example, allow 160 planning hours in a month. A complete trip cycle takes 16 hours of driving, loading, unloading, waiting and administration combined. That permits at most ten whole cycles in the planning model.</p>
<p>If the cycle rises to 20 hours because of additional waiting, only eight whole cycles fit the same allowance. Using the earlier $630 modeled remainder per trip solely as a constant sensitivity, ten cycles produce $6,300 and eight produce $5,040, a <strong>$1,260 reduction</strong>. Real waiting may also change driver cost, fuel and other inputs, so a full scenario must recalculate them.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Separate operating-time sensitivity</caption><thead><tr><th>Cycle time</th><th>Whole cycles within 160 hours</th><th>At unchanged $630 per cycle</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>16 hours</td><td>10</td><td>$6,300</td></tr><tr><td>20 hours</td><td>8</td><td>$5,040</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>This is not a lawful driving-hours schedule or a claim that a route can be completed in those times. Verify applicable operating requirements and actual lane evidence separately. Add customer/load sources, equipment needs, funding and contingency decisions to the <a href="/tools/business-plan-template/">written business plan</a>. Use the <a href="/journal/cash-flow-forecast-template-example/">cash forecast</a> for when fuel, payroll and collections actually move; an allocated per-trip remainder is not automatically cash available today.</p>''')


def main():
    scope = json.loads((HERE / 'scope.json').read_text())
    assert set(UPDATES) == {x['slug'] for x in scope}, 'Refresh must cover the exact GSC journal scope'
    before = {}
    for slug, update in UPDATES.items():
        path = ROOT / 'journal' / slug / 'index.html'
        source = path.read_text()
        assert 'id="nov10-' not in source, f'{slug}: already refreshed'
        published = re.findall(r'"datePublished"\s*:\s*"([^"]+)"', source)
        before[slug] = dict(datePublished=published,
                            title=re.search(r'<title>(.*?)</title>', source, re.S)[1],
                            description=re.search(r'<meta name="description" content="([^"]+)"', source)[1])
        section = '\n<section id="nov10-' + slug + '">\n<h2>' + html.escape(update['heading']) + '</h2>\n' + update['body'] + '\n</section>\n'
        # Keep substantive additions before the article's references/FAQ/summary,
        # rather than accumulating more detached paragraphs after </article>.
        article_end = source.index('</article>')
        anchor = re.search(r'<h2\b[^>]*>(?:Sources and method|Reference and example scope|Frequently asked[^<]*|FAQs|Common questions|Key takeaways|Monthly planner FAQs|A5 planner FAQs|2026 planner FAQs)', source[:article_end], re.I)
        pos = anchor.start() if anchor else article_end
        source = source[:pos] + section + source[pos:]
        source = re.sub(r'("dateModified"\s*:\s*")[^"]+("\s*[,}])', lambda m:m[1]+DATE+m[2], source)
        source = re.sub(r'(<meta\b[^>]*property="article:modified_time"[^>]*content=")[^"]+("[^>]*>)', lambda m:m[1]+DATE+m[2], source)
        assert re.findall(r'"datePublished"\s*:\s*"([^"]+)"', source) == published
        path.write_text(source)
    (HERE / 'before-metadata.json').write_text(json.dumps(before, indent=2)+'\n')
    (HERE / 'changes.json').write_text(json.dumps({k:{'heading':v['heading'],'gap':v['gap']} for k,v in UPDATES.items()}, indent=2)+'\n')
    print(f'Updated {len(UPDATES)} journals; original publication dates and page titles retained.')


if __name__ == '__main__':
    main()
